[Chapter "Main line (4.Nc3 dxc4)"]
[Line "Classical: 6.e3 and Qe2"]
[Opening "Slav Defense: Czech Variation, Classical System, Main Line"]

{The most played main line. Black develops quickly and calmly while White regains the c4 pawn.} 1. d4 d5 {Claim the centre.} 2. c4 {The Queen's Gambit: White attacks d5.} 2... c6 {The Slav: support d5 with a pawn and keep the c8 bishop's diagonal open, unlike ...e6.} 3. Nf3 Nf6 {Develop and guard d5 again.} 4. Nc3 dxc4 {Take on c4. White will win the pawn back, but meanwhile Black gets ...Bf5 in.} 5. a4 {Stops ...b5, which would hold on to the pawn.} 5... Bf5 {The light-squared bishop comes out before ...e6: the key Slav idea.} 6. e3 e6 {Now ...e6: the bishop is already outside the pawn chain.} 7. Bxc4 Bb4 {Pin the c3 knight, which makes White's e3–e4 harder to arrange.} 8. O-O O-O {Castle.} 9. Qe2 {White prepares e3–e4, which would hit the bishop on f5.} 9... Bg6 {Step away in advance, so e4 no longer comes with a gain of time.} *
